  "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+\u22ef+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(4+x)}^{9},$ we have $a=4,$ $b=x,$ and $n=9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+x)^{9}=(\\frac{9}{0}){4}^{9}{x}^{0}+(\\frac{9}{1}){4}^{9-1}{x}^{1}+(\\frac{9}{2}){4}^{9-2}{x}^{2}+\u22ef+(\\frac{9}{9}){4}^{9-9}{x}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{7}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{9}{7}){4}^{9-7}{x}^{7}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{9}{7}){4}^{9-7} & =36\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =36\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =576.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{7}$ is $576.$",
